# BSA M21 600 cc side-valve motorcycle with Watsonian Maxstoke sidecar.
#
# The motorcycle-and-sidecar outfit was the characteristic Very-low motorised
# vehicle of c.1920-1965. This object is the post-war family outfit: a
# purpose-built sidecar machine with a two-seat chair. It is a citycar object,
# so it carries no capacity field; the chair's two seats plus the pillion are
# represented by the class and comfort values only.
#
# Motorcycle: BSA M21, 591 cc side-valve single, 15 bhp, introduced 1937,
# production suspended 1940, resumed 1946, catalogued to 1958 and available to
# special order until 1963. Telescopic forks from 1948, optional plunger rear
# suspension from 1951, Amal Monobloc carburettor from 1955, 8 in front brake
# from 1956. Sidecar gearing, heavier flywheels and a wider rear tyre were the
# sidecar specification.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BSA_M21
#
# Sidecar: Watsonian Maxstoke two-seater saloon, adult in front and a child up
# to 4 ft 6 in in the rear compartment, all-steel fixed head, full-length door,
# 23 in body depth, 19.5 in front seat, 20 in rear seat, 16 in door (Watsonian
# 1945 description); the 1959 brochure lists the Maxstoke as a two-seater saloon
# on the VG chassis. Sidecar on the motorcycle's left, the British nearside.
# https://www.cybermotorcycle.com/gallery/watsonian/Watsonian-1959-Brochure-p06.htm
# https://www.cybermotorcycle.com/marques/watsonian/watsonian-1945.htm
#
# Dimensions: overall 2.06 m long x 1.50 m wide. Source: Science Museum Group
# object 1968-611, a 1958 AA M21 outfit with box sidecar, overall
# 1580 x 1500 x 2060 mm, 313 kg. The 1.58 m height belongs to that box body with
# canopy; the Maxstoke fixed head is lower (about 1.13 m), measured from the
# 1959 brochure. Motorcycle wheelbase about 1.35 m, measured from the side
# profile; wheels 19 in (3.25 front, 3.50 rear, 2.25 sidecar).
# https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co25295/m21-motorcycle-and-sidecar
#
# Colours: Black is the standard BSA finish (the photographed 1956 machine has a
# black frame and cycle parts); the red/maroon tank is the finish of surviving
# machines (1954 and 1956 examples); BSA Light Green is the Watsonian body
# finish listed for BSA outfits in the 1945 Watsonian range, and the 1959
# brochure lists duo-colour and single-colour schemes to the customer's choice.
# The tank, sidecar body and fixed head carry the paint colour; frame, guards,
# tyres and engine are black, and rims and exhaust are chrome.
#
# Colour shares and weight: no model-level outfit statistics exist. The outfit
# parc reached about 130,000-150,000 by 1952, roughly one in seven of the
# motorcycle parc (motorcycles 1,475,101 in 1958), against a Morris Minor parc
# of roughly 300,000 UK cars in the mid-1950s. The anchor model (Morris Minor)
# is 500 on the 2026-11-15 scale, so the outfit is set at about half the
# anchor: 240 in total. Black dominates at 140, maroon 60, BSA Light Green 40.
# Provisional pending the central rebase, as with all existing citycars.
#
# Speed 72 km/h (45 mph): a game-balance figure; a road test of an M21 outfit
# reached about 40 mph. Comfort 25/190 sits well below the BMC Mini's 40/240
# (open chair, exposed rider), with the tolerable time deliberately below the
# 180 + 1.5 x comfortable line for the same reason.
#
# Dates describe when outfits were seen regularly on UK roads, not production
# dates. The M21 resumed production in 1946; outfits were still common in the
# late 1950s and faded through the early 1960s as cheap cars replaced them
# (the Road Traffic Act 1960 barred learners from machines over 250 cc from
# July 1961). Retire dates are staggered by colour.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/bsa-m21-combo.blend in the blends repository.
# References: citycars/references/bsa-m21-watsonian/.
# - 1956 outfit, Lakeland Motor Museum: Thomas's Pics, CC BY 2.0, Wikimedia
#   Commons "1956 BSA M21 and Watsonian Sidecar (14850596948).jpg".
# - 1956 solo side profile: Yesterdays Antique Motorcycles, CC BY-SA 4.0,
#   Wikimedia Commons "1956 BSA M21 600cc-side valve motorcycle.jpg".
# - Watsonian 1959 brochure, Maxstoke page: scan from Sheldon's EMU
#   (cybermotorcycle.com); Watsonian copyright, research reference only.
# Stage B note: the 2.79 rig centres the combined bounding box; for this outfit a
# further +0.339 pak-unit lateral shift was applied after preparation so that the
# motorcycle, not the chair, sits at the tile centre. Re-render with that shift.

obj=citycar
name=BSAM21Combo(Black)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=140
intro_year=1946
intro_month=1
retire_year=1966
retire_month=6
speed=72
class=0,1
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=25
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=190

